.NET collections are mainly divided into two categories:

Non-Generic Collections
  - System.Collections
    - ArrayList, HashTable, Stack, Quue
Generic Collection
  - System.Collections.Generic
    - List<T>, Dictionary<TKey, TValue>, Stack<T>, Queue<T>, LinkedList<T>

Benefits of Generic Collections
- Type Safety - No runtime casting errors
- Performance - No boxing/unboxing
- Cleaner Code - Strong typing

1. List<T>
List<T> is an array-based dynamic collection.
List<int> numbers = new List<int>();
numbers.Add(10);
numbers.Add(20);
numbers.Add(30);

Memory is contiguous like an array.
When to Use List<T>
- Fast Indexing
- Mostly reading data - ECommerce, API Response

2. LinkedList<T>
LinkedList<T> is node based.
10 ↔ 20 ↔ 30 ↔ 40
When to Use LinkedList
- Use it when you have frequent insertions or removals in the middle.

Real World
- LRU Cache (Least Recently Used)
- Undo/Redo systems

3. Queue<T>
Queue follows FIFO (First In First Out).
When to Use Queue
- Use it for processing tasks in order.
- Real world
  - Job Processing System
  - Request Buffering - API Gateway, rate limiter
  - Breadth First Search

4. Stack<T>
Stack follows LIFO (Last In First Out).
When to Use Stack
- Use when the most recent item must be processed first.
- Real world
  - Undo operation
  - Function call
    Main()
    → FunctionA()
      → FunctionB()
